PAGE instructions
LAYOUT prose
TITLE Instructions
SUBTITLE Twenty minutes to set up, four minutes a morning to run
BLOCK Choose your size
TEXT Four sizes are included and all four contain identical pages. A4 and US Letter suit the desk. A5 and Half Letter suit a bedside table or a tablet, and print two-up on a full sheet.
BLOCK Set up once
ORDERED
ITEM Read the Morning Routine Guide
ITEM Write the wake time you can actually hold on a weekday
ITEM Build the sequence — three items, not twelve
ITEM Write the short version for bad mornings on the same page
ITEM Fill one Meditation and one Breathing page so you are not deciding at 06:00
BLOCK Every morning
ORDERED
ITEM Wake-Up Tracker — one tick, before anything else
ITEM Vision for the Day and Daily Intentions — four minutes
ITEM Run the sequence without renegotiating it
ITEM Morning Reflection last, in the final minute
BLOCK Every week and month
ORDERED
ITEM Weekly Morning Review — which mornings held, and what broke the others
ITEM Routine Consistency Tracker — mark the month
ITEM Monthly Progress Review and one line on the Success Dashboard
BLOCK Digital use
ORDERED
ITEM Import the PDF as a document, not as an image
ITEM Switch to read mode to tap links, write mode to write
ITEM Use the tabs to reach any section from any page

PAGE guide
TYPE guide
LAYOUT prose
TITLE Morning Routine Guide
SUBTITLE Six rules, and the reason behind each
CHIPS vision
BLOCK The wake time is the whole routine
TEXT Everything downstream depends on it, and it is the only part that cannot be made up later. A routine with a floating start is a list of intentions.
BLOCK Decide the night before, execute in the morning
TEXT Decisions are expensive at 06:00 and cheap at 22:00. The sequence, the clothes, the first task — all of it belongs to last night. The morning is for doing, not choosing.
BLOCK Body before mind
ITEM Water and movement first, they are cheap and they work
ITEM Mind work second, once you are actually awake
ITEM Screens last, and only after the sequence is finished
ITEM The phone in the first ten minutes ends the routine
BLOCK Write the short version in advance
TEXT Every routine meets a bad night, a sick child, an early flight. A three-minute version written in advance is what keeps the streak alive; improvising one at the time is how routines end.
BLOCK Track the wake, not the feeling
TEXT Whether you got up when you said is a fact. Whether the morning felt good is weather. The tracker records the fact.
BLOCK Add nothing for two weeks
TEXT The urge to expand a routine peaks in week one and is the most common cause of its collapse in week three. Fourteen unbroken days first, then one addition.

PAGE why
TYPE why
LAYOUT prose
TITLE Why Morning Routines Matter
SUBTITLE What the habit is actually buying you
CHIPS energy
BLOCK Decision load
TEXT Every unmade decision costs something small, and mornings are dense with them. A fixed sequence removes twenty of them before the day starts, which is most of the benefit people attribute to willpower.
BLOCK Compounding
TEXT Twenty minutes of reading is unremarkable. Twenty minutes held for a year is roughly a hundred and twenty hours, which is a serious body of work acquired at a time nobody was competing for.
BLOCK A buffer against a bad day
TEXT If the meaningful thing happens before nine, a day that collapses at eleven has still produced something. This is the single most useful property of a morning routine and the one people notice last.
BLOCK What it does not do
TEXT It will not fix a job you hate, and it will not compensate for six hours of sleep. Both of those need addressing directly. A morning routine built on insufficient sleep is a way of borrowing energy at a poor rate.

